Q: SQL Assertion statement A: <p>Oracle AI Database <a href="https://docs.oracle.com/en/database/oracle/oracle-database/26/nfcoa/ru23_26_1.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer">added support for assertions in RU 23.26.1</a>.</p>
<p>This enables you to <a href="https://docs.oracle.com/en/database/oracle/oracle-database/26/sqlrf/create-assertion.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer">create an assertion</a> like:</p>
<pre><code>create table employees (
  EmployeeName varchar2(30),
  Salary number,
  DeptName varchar2(30)
);

create table departments (
  DeptName varchar2(30),
  ManagerName varchar2(30),
  City varchar2(30)
);

create assertion sf_emps_earn_less_than_mgr
check (
  all ( 
    select * from employees e, departments d 
    where  e.DeptName = d.DeptName 
    and    d.city = 'San Francisco'
    and    d.DeptName != 'Management'
  ) sfemps
  satisfy (
    not exists (
      select * from employees mgr, departments d 
      where  mgr.DeptName = d.DeptName
      and    d.DeptName = 'Management'
      and    mgr.salary &lt; sfemps.salary
    )
  )
);

insert into departments values ( 'SF Dept', 'SF Manager', 'San Francisco' );
insert into departments values ( 'Management', 'SF Manager', 'San Francisco' );
insert into employees values ( 'SF Grunt', 50000, 'SF Dept');
insert into employees values ( 'SF Manager', 80000, 'Management');
insert into employees values ( 'SF Higher Manager', 90000, 'Management');

commit;

update employees 
set    salary = 90000
where  EmployeeName = 'SF Grunt';

--ORA-08601: SQL assertion (CHRIS.SF_EMPS_EARN_LESS_THAN_MGR) violated.

insert into employees values ( 'Overpaid Grunt', 90000, 'SF Dept');

--ORA-08601: SQL assertion (CHRIS.SF_EMPS_EARN_LESS_THAN_MGR) violated.
</code></pre>
<p>Some notes:</p>
<ul>
<li><p><code>all - satisfy</code> is a new condition. This condition is only available in assertions.</p>
</li>
<li><p>Subqueries are unsupported, so you can't compare to the <code>min ( salary )</code> for managers.</p>
</li>
<li><p><code>join on</code> syntax is unsupported in assertions</p>
</li>
</ul>

Q: Will the awakened thread be able to read the latest data if write operations are performed on the data without locking before notify_one? A: <p>The first problem is that your code can deadlock.</p>
<p>While we can look at it from a formal perspective, the cargo-cult rule for condition variables is:</p>
<p>You <em>must</em> hold the lock <em>at some point</em> between modifying the waited upon state and invoking notify.  Otherwise the waiting logic can miss the state change.</p>
<pre><code>cv.wait(lock, [] { return ready; });
</code></pre>
<p>this can read <code>ready</code>, then lose priority while holding <code>lock</code> before waiting for a <code>notify</code>.  If another thread can modify <code>ready</code> to convert it to the &quot;you should go&quot; and then <code>notify</code> in this time, the <code>notify</code> can be missed.</p>
<p><code>notify</code> does not produce a queue or increase a semaphore.  It only wakes up things <em>currently waiting</em> in the hardware.</p>
<p>We can fix this hole with:</p>
<pre><code>std::this_thread::sleep_for(std::chrono::milliseconds(100));
data = 42;
ready = true;
{  std::unique_lock&lt;std::mutex&gt; lock(mtx); }
cv.notify_one();
</code></pre>
<p>which makes modifying both <code>ready</code> and then <code>notify</code> impossible in that &quot;gap&quot; in the condition variable polling.</p>
<p>This leads to the second problem.  In C++ it is illegal to both modify and read data without synchronization; the result is undefined behavior.  UB here could result in the compiler making extremely unexpected optimizations, reading impossible values, or other strange things; the compiler is <em>free to assume</em> you never did that mistake.</p>
<p>And even if you look at the generated assembly from the code today and prove that this isn't a problem on todays hardware, tomorrow's rebuild with a newer compiler could break your assumptions, and a fresh cpu with different micro optimizations could as well.  When they decide if a new feature should be done, they look at the formal specs of the assembly (what did they promise), and on the compiler side on the language rules.  Only after that they might take a look at &quot;does this break every program&quot; (the defence of being wrong, but wrong in a common way)</p>
<p>Make your <code>bool</code> atomic to avoid this problem.</p>
<p>The third problem then becomes &quot;can you see the old value of data on x86 hardware&quot;; the answer is yes.  Without any synchronization going on, <code>ready</code> and <code>data</code> could be on different cache lines, and loading <code>ready</code> from a cpu cache could leave <code>data</code> stale.  As writing to <code>data</code> or <code>ready</code> doesn't flush the cpu cache and neither does reading, you could accidentally see a newer version of <code>ready</code> than you see <code>data</code>.</p>
<p>Now, x86 memory barriers tend to be more crude than other platforms - &quot;refresh all cache lines&quot; and the like.  When acquiring or releasing the mutex the compiler <em>may</em> inject a memory barrier that prevents this from happening.</p>
